# Line endings are decided here, not by whatever core.autocrlf each machine has.
#
# This repository is written on Windows and runs on Linux: the API, the export
# sidecar and nginx all read these files on the VPS. LF everywhere is the only
# setting where both ends agree.
#
# TWO THINGS THIS PREVENTS, one of which has already happened.
#
# 1. A .fountain file checked out as CRLF and imported into cloud mode made the
#    document UNEDITABLE. A <textarea> normalises CRLF to LF in its value, so the
#    CRDT held 1430 characters the editor could not show, and every offset that
#    crossed between them - a typed edit, a comment anchor, a suggestion range -
#    came out wrong by the number of lines above it. The server now normalises on
#    the way in and the client heals existing documents, so this is defence in
#    depth rather than the fix; the fix is in docs.js and collab.js.
#
# 2. The deploy shell scripts are copied to the VPS and run there. Checked out
#    with CRLF, `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r` fails with "bad interpreter" - and they
#    are LF in this working tree only because they happened to be written rather
#    than checked out. A fresh clone on Windows would break them.

# Normalise everything to LF in the repository AND in the working tree.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Spelled out for the two cases above, so the reason is findable from the file
# rather than only from this comment.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.fountain  text eol=lf
*.spmd      text eol=lf

# Binary: never examined for line endings, never normalised.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.fdx  text eol=lf
